{"task_id": "HumanEval/47", "solution": "```python\ndef median(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return median of elements in the list l.\n    This function calculates the statistical median for a given list of numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    sorted_l = sorted(l)\n    n = len(sorted_l)\n\n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        return sorted_l[n // 2]\n    else:\n        mid1 = sorted_l[n // 2 - 1]\n        mid2 = sorted_l[n // 2]\n        return (mid1 + mid2) / 2.0\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/47", "solution": "```python\ndef median(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return median of elements in the list l.\n    >>> median([3, 1, 2, 4, 5])\n    3\n    >>> median([-10, 4, 6, 1000, 10, 20])\n    15.0\n    \"\"\"\n    sorted_l = sorted(l)\n    n = len(sorted_l)\n\n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        # For an odd number of elements, the median is the middle element.\n        return float(sorted_l[n // 2])\n    else:\n        # For an even number of elements, the median is the average of the two middle elements.\n        mid1 = sorted_l[n // 2 - 1]\n        mid2 = sorted_l[n // 2]\n        return (float(mid1) + float(mid2)) / 2.0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/46", "solution": "```python\ndef fib4(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the n-th element of the Fib4 number sequence efficiently.\n    This implementation avoids recursion for performance.\n    \"\"\"\n    FIB4_INITIAL_VALUES = {\n        0: 0,\n        1: 0,\n        2: 2,\n        3: 0\n    }\n\n    if n < 0:\n        # According to the design, raise ValueError for negative input\n        # The design specifically mentioned InvalidInputError, but the prompt\n        # does not mention it and typical Python practice would be ValueError\n        # for out-of-range arguments. Since the prompt signature doesn't include\n        # a custom exception, I will stick to built-in exceptions if no\n        # custom exceptions are defined within the function.\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be non-negative.\")\n\n    if n in FIB4_INITIAL_VALUES:\n        return FIB4_INITIAL_VALUES[n]\n\n    # Initialize a list for dynamic programming (iterative approach).\n    # This list will store fib4(0) through fib4(n).\n    fib_sequence = [0] * (n + 1)\n\n    # Populate the base cases from FIB4_INITIAL_VALUES.\n    fib_sequence[0] = FIB4_INITIAL_VALUES[0]\n    fib_sequence[1] = FIB4_INITIAL_VALUES[1]\n    fib_sequence[2] = FIB4_INITIAL_VALUES[2]\n    fib_sequence[3] = FIB4_INITIAL_VALUES[3]\n\n    # Calculate subsequent values using the recurrence relation.\n    for i in range(4, n + 1):\n        fib_sequence[i] = (\n            fib_sequence[i-1] +\n            fib_sequence[i-2] +\n            fib_sequence[i-3] +\n            fib_sequence[i-4]\n        )\n    return fib_sequence[n]\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/49", "solution": "def modp(n: int, p: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates 2^n modulo p using exponentiation by squaring.\n    This method efficiently handles large exponents and intermediate values.\n    \"\"\"\n    res = 1\n    base = 2 % p\n\n    while n > 0:\n        if n % 2 == 1:\n            res = (res * base) % p\n        base = (base * base) % p\n        n //= 2\n    return res"}
