{"task_id": "HumanEval/57", "solution": "```python\ndef _is_increasing(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a list is non-decreasing.\n    It returns False at the first instance where an element is greater than its successor.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(len(l) - 1):\n        if l[i] > l[i+1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef _is_decreasing(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a list is non-increasing.\n    It returns False at the first instance where an element is less than its successor.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(len(l) - 1):\n        if l[i] < l[i+1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef monotonic(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return True is list elements are monotonically increasing or decreasing.\n    >>> monotonic([1, 2, 4, 20])\n    True\n    >>> monotonic([1, 20, 4, 10])\n    False\n    >>> monotonic([4, 1, 0, -10])\n    True\n    \"\"\"\n    return _is_increasing(l) or _is_decreasing(l)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/59", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_prime_factor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the largest prime factor of a given integer n.\n    Assumes n > 1 and is not a prime.\n    \"\"\"\n    largest_factor = 1\n\n    # Handle factor 2\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        largest_factor = 2\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Handle odd factors\n    # Iterate through odd numbers starting from 3 up to the square root of n (effectively)\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= n:\n        while n % i == 0:\n            largest_factor = i\n            n //= i\n        i += 2\n\n    # If n is still greater than 2, it means the remaining n is a prime factor itself\n    if n > 2:\n        largest_factor = n\n\n    return largest_factor\n```"}
