{"task_id": "HumanEval/76", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef is_simple_power(x, n):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number x is a simple power of n.\n    Returns true if there exists an integer k such that n^k = x.\n    \"\"\"\n    tolerance = 1e-9\n\n    if x == 1.0:\n        return True\n    if n == 1.0 and x != 1.0:\n        return False\n    if n <= 0:\n        return False\n    if x < 0:\n        return False\n\n    try:\n        # Calculate k = log_n(x) = log(x) / log(n)\n        exponent = math.log(x) / math.log(n)\n        return abs(exponent - round(exponent)) < tolerance\n    except ValueError:\n        # Handles cases like math.log(0) if x happens to be 0 at this point.\n        return False\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/75", "solution": "```python\ndef is_multiply_prime(a):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given number 'a' is the product of exactly three prime numbers.\n    The input 'a' is an integer between 1 and 99 (inclusive).\n    Example:\n    is_multiply_prime(30) == True\n    30 = 2 * 3 * 5\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _is_prime(n):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a given integer n is a prime number.\"\"\"\n        if n < 2:\n            return False\n        for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    # As 'a' is less than 100, the largest possible prime factor\n    # (p3, where p1*p2*p3 = a and p1, p2 >= 2) will be at most a / (2 * 2) = a / 4.\n    # For a=99, the largest prime factor p3 <= 99/4 = 24.75.\n    # So, we only need to generate primes up to 23.\n    primes = [p for p in range(2, 24) if _is_prime(p)]\n\n    # Iterate through combinations of three primes (p1, p2, p3) allowing repetitions.\n    # This ensures p1 <= p2 <= p3, covering cases like 2*2*2=8 or 2*2*3=12.\n    for p1 in primes:\n        for p2 in primes:\n            if p2 < p1:\n                continue\n            for p3 in primes:\n                if p3 < p2:\n                    continue\n\n                product = p1 * p2 * p3\n\n                if product == a:\n                    return True\n\n                # Optimization: if the current product already exceeds 'a',\n                # further increasing p3 (which is sorted) will also exceed 'a'.\n                # So, we can break the inner loop.\n                if product > a:\n                    break\n\n    return False\n```"}
