{"task_id": "HumanEval/156", "solution": "def int_to_mini_roman(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer, obtain its roman numeral equivalent as a string,\n    and return it in lowercase.\n    Restrictions: 1 <= num <= 1000\n\n    Examples:\n    >>> int_to_mini_roman(19) == 'xix'\n    >>> int_to_mini_roman(152) == 'clii'\n    >>> int_to_mini_roman(426) == 'cdxxvi'\n    \"\"\"\n    ROMAN_NUMERAL_MAP = [\n        (1000, \"M\"), (900, \"CM\"), (500, \"D\"), (400, \"CD\"),\n        (100, \"C\"), (90, \"XC\"), (50, \"L\"), (40, \"XL\"),\n        (10, \"X\"), (9, \"IX\"), (5, \"V\"), (4, \"IV\"), (1, \"I\")\n    ]\n\n    roman_numeral = []\n    temp_num = number\n    for value, symbol in ROMAN_NUMERAL_MAP:\n        while temp_num >= value:\n            roman_numeral.append(symbol)\n            temp_num -= value\n    return \"\".join(roman_numeral).lower()"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "```python\ndef right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if three side lengths form a right-angled triangle.\n    It uses the Pythagorean theorem with tolerance for float precision.\n    \"\"\"\n    EPSILON = 1e-9 # Tolerance for floating point comparisons.\n\n    side1 = float(a)\n    side2 = float(b)\n    side3 = float(c)\n\n    sides = sorted([side1, side2, side3])\n\n    return abs(sides[0]**2 + sides[1]**2 - sides[2]**2) < EPSILON\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "```python\ndef find_max(words):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the string with the most unique characters from a list.\n    In case of a tie, the lexicographically smallest string is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not words:\n        return \"\"\n\n    max_unique_count = -1\n    result_word = \"\"\n\n    for word in words:\n        current_unique_count = len(set(word))\n\n        if current_unique_count > max_unique_count:\n            max_unique_count = current_unique_count\n            result_word = word\n        elif current_unique_count == max_unique_count:\n            # Tie-breaking: lexicographical order\n            if word < result_word:\n                result_word = word\n\n    return result_word\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "```python\ndef find_max(words):\n    \"\"\"Write a function that accepts a list of strings.\n    The list contains different words. Return the word with maximum number\n    of unique characters. If multiple strings have maximum number of unique\n    characters, return the one which comes first in lexicographical order.\n\n    find_max([\"name\", \"of\", \"string\"]) == \"string\"\n    find_max([\"name\", \"enam\", \"game\"]) == \"enam\"\n    find_max([\"aaaaaaa\", \"bb\" ,\"cc\"]) == \"aaaaaaa\"\n    \"\"\"\n    max_unique_count = -1\n    result_word = \"\"\n\n    for word in words:\n        current_unique_count = len(set(word))\n\n        if current_unique_count > max_unique_count:\n            max_unique_count = current_unique_count\n            result_word = word\n        elif current_unique_count == max_unique_count:\n            # Tie-breaking: lexicographical order\n            if word < result_word:\n                result_word = word\n\n    return result_word\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/159", "solution": "def eat(number, need, remaining):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total carrots eaten and remaining stock after a rabbit's meal.\n    Returns `[total_eaten_carrots, carrots_left_in_stock]`.\n    \"\"\"\n    carrots_to_eat_from_stock = min(need, remaining)\n    total_eaten_carrots = number + carrots_to_eat_from_stock\n    carrots_left_in_stock = remaining - carrots_to_eat_from_stock\n    return [total_eaten_carrots, carrots_left_in_stock]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/156", "solution": "```python\ndef int_to_mini_roman(number: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a positive integer to its lowercase Roman numeral equivalent.\n    Assumes input `number` is an integer between 1 and 1000, inclusive.\n    \"\"\"\n    ROMAN_MAP = [\n        (1000, 'm'), (900, 'cm'), (500, 'd'), (400, 'cd'),\n        (100, 'c'), (90, 'xc'), (50, 'l'), (40, 'xl'),\n        (10, 'x'), (9, 'ix'), (5, 'v'), (4, 'iv'), (1, 'i')\n    ]\n\n    result = []\n    temp_num = number\n    for value, symbol in ROMAN_MAP:\n        while temp_num >= value:\n            result.append(symbol)\n            temp_num -= value\n    return \"\".join(result)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return True if the three\n    sides form a right-angled triangle, False otherwise.\n    A right-angled triangle is a triangle in which one angle is right angle or\n    90 degree.\n    Example:\n    right_angle_triangle(3, 4, 5) == True\n    right_angle_triangle(1, 2, 3) == False\n    '''\n    EPSILON = 1e-9  # Tolerance for floating-point comparisons.\n\n    # FR1: Input validation for positive numbers.\n    if not all(isinstance(side, (int, float)) and side > 0 for side in [a, b, c]):\n        raise ValueError(\"Side lengths must be positive numbers.\")\n\n    def _is_valid_triangle(s1: float, s2: float, s3: float) -> bool:\n        # Checks if the given side lengths can form a valid triangle using the\n        # triangle inequality theorem.\n        # Sum of any two sides must be greater than the third side.\n        return (s1 + s2 > s3) and (s1 + s3 > s2) and (s2 + s3 > s1)\n\n    # FR2: Check if the sides form a valid triangle.\n    if not _is_valid_triangle(a, b, c):\n        return False\n\n    # FR3: Apply Pythagorean theorem.\n    # Sort sides to easily identify the longest side (hypotenuse).\n    sides = sorted([a, b, c])\n    s1_squared = sides[0] ** 2\n    s2_squared = sides[1] ** 2\n    s3_squared = sides[2] ** 2 # This is the square of the longest side.\n\n    # Use math.isclose for robust floating-point comparison.\n    return math.isclose(s1_squared + s2_squared, s3_squared, rel_tol=EPSILON)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/159", "solution": "def eat(number, need, remaining):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total carrots eaten and remaining after a rabbit's meal.\n    The rabbit eats what it needs or all available carrots if stock is insufficient.\n    \"\"\"\n    if need <= remaining:\n        total_eaten = number + need\n        carrots_left = remaining - need\n    else:\n        total_eaten = number + remaining\n        carrots_left = 0\n\n    return [total_eaten, carrots_left]"}
